faq:
  - q: "What is LinkedIn's hiring process in 2026?"
    a: "LinkedIn's hiring process typically runs in four stages: (1) Recruiter screen — 20–30 minutes on background, motivations, and role fit. (2) Hiring manager interview — 45–60 minutes, behavioral + role-specific discussion. (3) Technical or skills assessment — for engineering roles, a take-home or live coding challenge; for product, a product case exercise; for sales, a pipeline and territory walkthrough. (4) Loop or panel interviews — 3–5 rounds with cross-functional stakeholders covering technical depth, behavioral questions, and cultural alignment. Total process typically takes 4–8 weeks."
  - q: "What does LinkedIn look for in candidates?"
    a: "LinkedIn looks for candidates who embody its culture of transformation, collaboration, and member-first thinking. Specific signals: (1) Member obsession — can you articulate how your work creates value for LinkedIn's 1B+ members? (2) Product thinking — even non-PM roles are expected to understand the product and its business model. (3) AI/data fluency — LinkedIn has invested heavily in AI-powered recruiting and sales tools; roles across all functions are increasingly expected to work with AI outputs. (4) Ownership and initiative — LinkedIn promotes from within aggressively and looks for candidates who drive impact beyond their immediate scope."
  - q: "How does LinkedIn engineering recruiting work?"
    a: "LinkedIn engineering interviews follow a modified FAANG structure. Recruiter screen → coding assessment (typically a take-home or HackerRank, medium difficulty) → hiring manager screen → 4–5 loop interviews covering: 2 coding rounds (LeetCode medium/hard), 1 system design round, 1 behavioral/values round, 1 domain-specific round for senior+ roles. LinkedIn is particularly known for asking distributed systems and data infrastructure questions — they operate one of the world's largest professional data graphs and their systems questions reflect that scale. Senior engineers should prepare for questions about data pipelines, search infrastructure, and feed ranking systems."
  - q: "What are LinkedIn product manager interviews like?"
    a: "LinkedIn PM interviews follow a standard structure with a LinkedIn-specific lens. Expect: product design questions (design a feature for LinkedIn recruiters, design a feed improvement for job seekers), metrics and analytical questions (how would you measure success of LinkedIn Learning?), strategy questions (LinkedIn's competitive position vs. Indeed, Glassdoor), and estimation questions (how many premium subscribers does LinkedIn have?). Strong PM candidates demonstrate deep LinkedIn product knowledge — use the product extensively, know the monetization model (Recruiter, Sales Navigator, Learning, Premium), and have informed opinions about what LinkedIn should build next."
  - q: "What does LinkedIn pay in 2026?"
    a: "LinkedIn compensation is slightly below peak FAANG (Google, Meta) but competitive for Microsoft-owned companies. Software Engineer L4: $180–220K TC; L5: $220–280K TC; L6: $280–380K TC; L7 (Principal): $400–500K+ TC. Product Manager L5: $220–280K TC; L6 (Senior PM): $280–380K TC; Director of PM: $380–500K+ TC. Sales roles (Account Executive for Talent Solutions): OTE $150–250K. Compensation is primarily base + RSU with standard Microsoft equity refresh cycles. LinkedIn's comp has compressed relative to pure-play Big Tech but remains strong for the Bay Area market."
  - q: "How do you get a LinkedIn referral?"
    a: "LinkedIn's own platform is the most effective tool for getting a LinkedIn referral. Identify LinkedIn employees in your target function through LinkedIn search, reach out via InMail or connection request with a brief, specific message explaining why you're interested in LinkedIn and what you bring, and ask if they'd be open to a 15-minute call. LinkedIn employees are often willing to give referrals for strong candidates because it benefits them (LinkedIn has a referral bonus program). Your LinkedIn profile itself will be evaluated as part of the referral process — a strong, complete, optimized profile is effectively a sample of your LinkedIn expertise."
